    "body": "A World Cup final can change with one moment. On July 19, 2026, Spain and Argentina met for the men's world championship at New York New Jersey Stadium. The score remained 0–0 through the regular ninety minutes. Players had spent the game running, defending, passing, and looking for an opening, but neither team had scored. Then, in extra time, Spanish substitute Ferran Torres found the decisive goal. Spain won 1–0 and became world champions for the second time.\n\n## What is extra time?\n\nSoccer matches normally include two forty-five-minute halves, plus added stoppage time. In knockout games where the rules require a winner, a tied match can continue into extra time. Extra time adds two fifteen-minute periods. Teams may be tired after so much running, but they must keep making decisions under pressure. A substitute, or player brought into the game from the bench, can offer fresh energy and a different approach. In this final, that mattered enormously.\n\n## One goal in the 106th minute\n\nThe official FIFA report describes an attacking move that led to the winner. Pedro Porro crossed the ball into the area, Nico Williams headed it down, and Ferran Torres struck it into the net. The goal came in the 106th minute, sixteen minutes after regulation ended. Argentina's goalkeeper, Emiliano Martínez, had stopped several earlier attempts. That made the winning goal especially dramatic: Spain had created opportunities but needed to keep trying before one succeeded.\n\n## The other side of the final\n\nArgentina had entered the tournament as the defending men's champions after winning in 2022. Lionel Messi was part of that history and played in another final four years later. A loss in the championship match can be painful for a team that has trained together and traveled far. Sports reporting should describe both teams fairly. Spain celebrated a major victory; Argentina finished as runners-up after reaching the last match of a demanding tournament. Both outcomes are part of the story.\n\n## A bigger tournament\n\nThe 2026 men's World Cup was the first with forty-eight participating national teams and 104 matches. Games took place in Canada, Mexico, and the United States. The larger format meant more national teams had the chance to compete on the biggest stage. Fans traveled to cities across North America, and people watched from around the world. Organizing a tournament of that size requires stadium workers, transportation teams, officials, volunteers, and countless others whose names never appear on the scoreboard.\n\n## A lesson about substitutions\n\nPlayers who begin a soccer match are called starters, but the substitutes are also part of the team. Coaches decide when to bring someone on based on energy, injuries, tactics, and the match situation. A fresh player might run into open space that tired defenders are slower to cover. That does not mean a substitute is less important than a starter. Sometimes a player's most important moment comes after sitting on the bench for much of the game. Ferran Torres's winning goal offers a memorable example. A sports team succeeds when people prepare for their opportunities, even if they do not know exactly when those opportunities will arrive.\n\nThe stadium, date, and scorer help readers distinguish one tournament from another. A student making a sports timeline can include these facts beside the result and explain why the goal mattered. Clear sources make the timeline stronger than a collection of rumors shared online.\n\n## Read the score carefully\n\nA final score of 1–0 says who won, but it does not tell every part of the match. It does not reveal how many saves a goalkeeper made or how hard a team worked to defend. A sports reporter combines the result with key events and reliable sources so readers understand why the outcome mattered. Spain's victory is a good example: one goal was enough, but the story includes preparation, perseverance, and two teams trying to reach the same prize.",
