    "slug": "hip-hop-born-in-the-bronx",
    "section": "Arts",
    "kind": "feature",
    "title": "Hip-Hop: How a Bronx Party Became a Global Culture",
    "dek": "DJs, dancers, rappers, and artists turned creative ideas in New York neighborhoods into a worldwide movement.",
    "src": [
      "https://www.smithsonianmag.com/arts-culture/hip-hop-history-180982727/",
      "https://nmaahc.si.edu/explore/stories/hip-hop"
    ],
    "body": "A neighborhood party can change the world in unexpected ways. In the 1970s, young people in the Bronx, a part of New York City, developed creative ways to use records, microphones, dance, and art. Their work helped shape hip-hop, a culture that eventually spread around the world. Hip-hop is more than one kind of music. It includes traditions of DJing, rapping, breaking, and visual art such as graffiti. Each grew from people using the tools and spaces available to express themselves.\n\n## The DJ controls the beat\n\nAt parties, DJs played records so people could dance. Some learned to focus on the break, a rhythmic section of a song that dancers especially enjoyed. By switching between two turntables, a DJ could extend the part of the music that kept people moving. One influential early figure was DJ Kool Herc, who organized a Bronx party in 1973 that became a famous landmark in hip-hop history. Many people contributed to the culture's beginnings, so its history should not be reduced to one person or one afternoon.\n\n## Words join the rhythm\n\nMCs, sometimes called masters of ceremonies, used microphones to guide parties and excite the crowd. Over time, spoken rhymes became more elaborate. Rappers developed patterns of rhythm, storytelling, humor, and wordplay. A strong rap verse depends on how words fit into the beat. The rhyme at the end of a line may be obvious, but performers also use sounds inside lines, pauses, and changes in speed. Those techniques connect rap to poetry, public speaking, and oral storytelling.\n\n## Dancing and art\n\nBreaking, sometimes called breakdancing, developed as dancers created athletic moves and friendly competitions. Crews practiced together and invented new combinations. Visual artists also shaped the culture through lettering, murals, and graffiti. These art forms can express identity, neighborhood pride, and creativity. Not every act of writing on a wall is legal or welcome, but the style of hip-hop lettering influenced design, fashion, and public art far beyond the Bronx.\n\n## A worldwide movement\n\nAs recordings, films, and performances spread, people in other cities and countries developed their own hip-hop scenes. Rappers perform in many languages, and local dancers bring their own musical influences into breaking. A tradition that began in New York became a conversation among communities across the globe. Understanding that history means giving attention to the Black and Latino young people who were central to the culture's early development.\n\n## Turn a pattern into a poem\n\nRhythm and language can work together in surprising ways. Take the short sentence, \"I walk to school,\" and say it over four steady claps. Then replace one word or add a phrase while keeping the beat. You may need to pause or shorten a word. Rappers make similar decisions when fitting language into musical timing, although professional performers use far more complex patterns. A rhyme does not need to appear only at the end of a line. Words with matching sounds can create patterns inside a sentence too. Studying those choices helps explain why hip-hop is an expressive art form and a valuable subject for reading and music classes.\n\nMusic history becomes clearer when students look at both artists and the places they lived. A map of New York shows the Bronx as one of the city's boroughs. Photographs and interviews can help reveal the community centers, parks, and parties where early artists met. Asking who participated, what tools they used, and what problems they were solving creates a richer history than memorizing a single famous name.\n\n## Hear the layers\n\nListen to a school-appropriate hip-hop track and identify the beat, the repeated sounds, and the pattern of the words. Then write four original lines about a place you know. Try speaking them over a steady clap without copying anyone else's lyrics. Hip-hop history shows how creativity often begins when people share skills, adapt available tools, and build something new together.",
